WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.072
In this film you read the fund part of a FundsXML document: the fund, its net asset value and its share classes, and you check an identifier.

2
00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:10.723
It takes about six minutes.

3
00:00:11.592 --> 00:00:13.669
Below Funds comes the fund itself.

4
00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:17.673
It is identified by its LEI, the global Legal Entity Identifier.

5
00:00:17.723 --> 00:00:21.176
It has an official name, and a fund currency, here euro.

6
00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:28.684
The fund currency matters: the fund's total values are given in it, and the positions of its portfolio are valued in it.

7
00:00:30.345 --> 00:00:33.331
The SingleFundFlag decides the structure below.

8
00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:37.335
True means a single fund, whose share classes follow directly.

9
00:00:37.385 --> 00:00:43.666
False means an umbrella fund, which contains subfunds, and each subfund has its own share classes.

10
00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:47.160
Here are the two shapes side by side.

11
00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:52.144
A single fund has one portfolio and a list of share classes below SingleFund.

12
00:00:52.194 --> 00:00:56.545
An umbrella, for example a SICAV, lists its subfunds below Subfunds.

13
00:00:56.595 --> 00:01:02.629
Each subfund has its own static and dynamic data, its own portfolio and its own share classes.

14
00:01:02.679 --> 00:01:08.907
The building blocks are the same in both shapes, so code that reads a share class works for both.

15
00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:12.574
FundStaticData holds what rarely changes.

16
00:01:12.624 --> 00:01:17.389
Here, the domicile, Austria, the legal structure, UCITS, and the inception date.

17
00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:25.754
Further down follow the companies and managers, and the ongoing charges of one point five eight percent, with the date they were published.

18
00:01:26.904 --> 00:01:35.586
Static data can hold much more: the fund's legal data and domicile, its managers, its benchmarks, its classification, and its costs.

19
00:01:35.636 --> 00:01:37.107
Most of it is optional.

20
00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:43.127
A delivery fills what its receivers need, and sender and receiver agree on that in advance.

21
00:01:44.377 --> 00:01:47.817
FundDynamicData holds what changes with every valuation.

22
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:50.496
Total asset values list one entry per date.

23
00:01:50.546 --> 00:01:56.416
This one is for the thirty-first of October; its nature is official, as opposed to an estimate.

24
00:01:56.466 --> 00:02:00.654
The total net asset value is about two hundred seventy-nine million.

25
00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:04.393
And every amount carries its currency, in the attribute ccy.

26
00:02:05.542 --> 00:02:08.015
This pattern is used everywhere in FundsXML.

27
00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:11.514
An amount is a number with its currency in the attribute ccy.

28
00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.954
The same amount may appear several times, once per currency.

29
00:02:15.004 --> 00:02:20.746
And optional flags mark which one is in the fund currency, and which one in the share class currency.

30
00:02:20.796 --> 00:02:24.875
So a receiver never has to guess the currency, or convert a value again.

31
00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:28.398
Further down are the share classes.

32
00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:32.579
The first one has its own ISIN, a name, and its currency, euro.

33
00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:35.100
Its type says retail and accumulating.

34
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:45.785
Its prices are a daily history, one entry per business day: an action code C, for create, a NAV date, the price currency, the nature official, and the NAV price.

35
00:02:45.835 --> 00:02:51.226
On the thirty-first of October: one hundred twenty-eight euro and forty-one cents.

36
00:02:52.248 --> 00:02:55.262
Now the dollar class, hedged against the euro.

37
00:02:55.312 --> 00:03:04.854
It gives its total net asset value twice: in its own currency, dollar, marked isShareClassCcy, and in the fund currency, euro, marked isFundCcy.

38
00:03:04.904 --> 00:03:08.984
About four hundred sixty-five thousand shares are outstanding.

39
00:03:09.034 --> 00:03:12.847
Multiply them by the price, and you get the dollar amount.

40
00:03:13.498 --> 00:03:17.872
The second share class is for institutional investors, in euro as well.

41
00:03:17.922 --> 00:03:24.166
It has its own ISIN, its own name, and a much higher price: one thousand one hundred eighty-six euro.

42
00:03:24.216 --> 00:03:32.143
This fund has four share classes: retail and institutional in euro, and two retail classes hedged into Swiss francs and dollars.

43
00:03:32.193 --> 00:03:38.125
The same fund, the same portfolio, but different currencies, prices, fees and numbers of shares.

44
00:03:38.175 --> 00:03:40.805
That is exactly what share classes are for.

45
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:55.508
A share class can carry much more: whether it distributes or accumulates income, whether it is currency hedged, minimum investments, the countries where it is registered, its fees, and performance figures.

46
00:03:55.558 --> 00:04:02.476
Action codes create, modify or delete entries, so a delivery can also update data that a receiver already has.

47
00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:05.385
Names deserve a word, too.

48
00:04:05.435 --> 00:04:10.952
Every fund and every share class has an official name, the legal name from the prospectus.

49
00:04:11.002 --> 00:04:15.344
Short names and marketing names can follow where a receiver needs them.

50
00:04:15.394 --> 00:04:22.582
And texts can be given in several languages side by side, so one delivery can serve receivers in different countries.

51
00:04:22.632 --> 00:04:27.035
Do not build your keys on names, though: names change, identifiers stay.

52
00:04:28.385 --> 00:04:31.858
Identifiers are the key to every match between systems.

53
00:04:31.908 --> 00:04:34.357
Share classes and securities use ISINs.

54
00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:39.161
Companies, such as the fund, the management company or an issuer, use LEIs.

55
00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:42.555
National codes and vendor codes can be given as well.

56
00:04:42.605 --> 00:04:48.448
Both ISIN and LEI end in check digits, so a typing error can be found without asking anyone.

57
00:04:49.698 --> 00:04:52.247
Is the ISIN of this share class correct?

58
00:04:52.297 --> 00:04:56.469
Open the FundsXML panel, and type it into the identifier checker.

59
00:04:56.519 --> 00:05:01.016
The checker knows the structure and the check digit of ISINs and LEIs.

60
00:05:01.066 --> 00:05:02.185
This one is valid.

61
00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:07.381
The letters A T in front stand for Austria; X M P L marks it as an example code.

62
00:05:09.091 --> 00:05:12.308
Change a single digit, and the check digit no longer fits.

63
00:05:12.358 --> 00:05:15.970
Typing errors like this are caught before the data goes anywhere.

64
00:05:16.624 --> 00:05:18.019
The same works for LEIs.

65
00:05:18.069 --> 00:05:22.415
Paste the LEI of the fund, and the checker confirms its two check digits.

66
00:05:22.465 --> 00:05:28.737
For a whole document, the button Check Identifiers Online looks up all its codes in the public registers.

67
00:05:28.787 --> 00:05:36.143
The codes of the example files are made up; FreeXmlToolkit recognises them by the X M P L marker and does not look them up.

68
00:05:38.026 --> 00:05:40.595
A few tips for code that reads fund data.

69
00:05:40.645 --> 00:05:45.451
Read both shapes: single funds and umbrellas, decided by the SingleFundFlag.

70
00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:54.077
Use the date of each entry: every price and total value has its own NAV date, and the content date of the delivery is only the default.

71
00:05:54.127 --> 00:05:57.655
Always read the currency attribute, and never assume it.

72
00:05:57.705 --> 00:06:04.300
And expect lists: several dates, several prices, and names in several languages can appear side by side.

73
00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.916
That was the fund part of a delivery.

74
00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:12.110
In the next film, you read the portfolio and the asset master data.

75
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.363
Thanks for watching.
